1
00:00:00,464 --> 00:00:02,165
In the beginning,

2
00:00:02,165 --> 00:00:06,870
there were shapeless spirits roaming the universe.

3
00:00:06,870 --> 00:00:09,940
And they went to the creator and said,

4
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:11,141
"We are lonely.

5
00:00:11,141 --> 00:00:13,176
Please help us."

6
00:00:13,176 --> 00:00:14,578
And the creator said,

7
00:00:14,578 --> 00:00:17,748
"I will make you a place to be together,

8
00:00:17,748 --> 00:00:19,850
on one condition.

9
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:23,186
You must take care of it."

10
00:00:23,186 --> 00:00:25,722
The spirits agreed.

11
00:00:25,722 --> 00:00:28,659
And the creator made the Earth

12
00:00:28,659 --> 00:00:31,461
and sent the spirits down to the Earth

13
00:00:31,461 --> 00:00:35,165
to become land and water, plants,

14
00:00:35,165 --> 00:00:37,301
animals and humans,

15
00:00:37,301 --> 00:00:41,805
making everything on Earth alive.

16
00:00:41,805 --> 00:00:44,274
The creator told my ancestors,

17
00:00:44,274 --> 00:00:46,877
"You will never want for anything,

18
00:00:46,877 --> 00:00:51,448
so long as you live in a balance with the natural world.

19
00:00:51,448 --> 00:00:54,017
Never take more than what you need.

20
00:00:54,017 --> 00:00:59,823
Give thanks. And protect what gives you life.”

21
00:00:59,823 --> 00:01:06,730
That is a version of my nation, the Yurok tribe's, creation story.

22
00:01:06,730 --> 00:01:09,766
It's not spiritual parable.

23
00:01:09,766 --> 00:01:11,868
It's a set of instructions.

24
00:01:11,868 --> 00:01:13,937
Simple, practical rules

25
00:01:13,937 --> 00:01:19,276
about how humans are meant to live on Earth.

26
00:01:19,276 --> 00:01:23,680
I want to tell you a story of loss,

27
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:26,617
resilience and victory,

28
00:01:26,617 --> 00:01:30,120
and what it means to remember.

29
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:36,627
Since time immemorial, my nation has lived by these instructions.

30
00:01:36,627 --> 00:01:38,328
The lower Klamath River,

31
00:01:38,328 --> 00:01:41,565
in what is now known as Northern California,

32
00:01:41,565 --> 00:01:44,401
is our home and our lifeblood.

33
00:01:44,401 --> 00:01:46,470
Under our stewardship,

34
00:01:46,470 --> 00:01:50,407
the Klamath salmon runs grew to be the third largest

35
00:01:50,407 --> 00:01:53,143
in all the continental US.

36
00:01:53,143 --> 00:01:57,214
We had an abundant fishing way of life,

37
00:01:57,214 --> 00:02:01,018
caring for all that gave us life.

38
00:02:01,018 --> 00:02:03,820
Then in the 1800s, everything changed.

39
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:07,024
Newcomers arrived, and they viewed our world

40
00:02:07,024 --> 00:02:09,426
through the lens of extraction:

41
00:02:09,426 --> 00:02:13,196
gold, timber, water, land and people.

42
00:02:13,196 --> 00:02:18,602
The balance was disrupted, and suffering ensued in this place.

43
00:02:18,602 --> 00:02:20,203
By 1910,

44
00:02:20,203 --> 00:02:25,909
over 90 percent of California's Indigenous population had been slaughtered.

45
00:02:25,909 --> 00:02:28,512
It was genocide.

46
00:02:28,512 --> 00:02:29,946
By the 2000s,

47
00:02:29,946 --> 00:02:33,283
the Klamath River, our ancient relative,

48
00:02:33,283 --> 00:02:35,652
had been transformed by dams,

49
00:02:35,652 --> 00:02:38,922
habitat loss, water diversions

50
00:02:38,922 --> 00:02:42,225
and warming polluted waters.

51
00:02:42,225 --> 00:02:44,461
The dams, built without fish passage,

52
00:02:44,461 --> 00:02:47,964
blocked the salmon from accessing over 400 miles

53
00:02:47,964 --> 00:02:50,767
of historical spawning grounds.

54
00:02:50,767 --> 00:02:55,872
Our salmon runs fell to five percent of their historical abundance.

55
00:02:55,872 --> 00:03:00,577
The hydropower from the dams wasn't green either.

56
00:03:00,577 --> 00:03:03,880
The reservoirs behind the dams emitted methane,

57
00:03:03,880 --> 00:03:10,554
the equivalent to 64,000 cars a year on the road.

58
00:03:10,554 --> 00:03:14,725
In 2002, the legacy of colonization

59
00:03:14,725 --> 00:03:17,761
produced a horrific outcome.

60
00:03:17,761 --> 00:03:22,065
Over 70,000 adult salmon died on the Klamath River

61
00:03:22,065 --> 00:03:24,234
within the Yurok Reservation,

62
00:03:24,234 --> 00:03:28,071
the largest salmon kill in US history.

63
00:03:28,071 --> 00:03:31,241
I was working for tribal fisheries that summer,

64
00:03:31,241 --> 00:03:33,744
and the salmon died for two weeks.

65
00:03:33,744 --> 00:03:35,345
By the end of it,

66
00:03:35,345 --> 00:03:38,882
their bodies lined the banks of the river,

67
00:03:38,882 --> 00:03:43,954
and the river looked and smelled like a war zone.

68
00:03:43,954 --> 00:03:50,494
Standing in a Yurok tribal fisheries boat, I recalled a Yurok myth.

69
00:03:50,494 --> 00:03:54,564
If the Klamath salmon die, so too will the Yurok people.

70
00:03:54,564 --> 00:03:59,770
Because our purpose, our way of life depends on those salmon.

71
00:03:59,770 --> 00:04:03,240
Was I witnessing the end?

72
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:04,641
In that moment,

73
00:04:04,641 --> 00:04:09,746
I felt my great grandmother move through me like electricity.

74
00:04:09,746 --> 00:04:11,548
I feel it now.

75
00:04:11,548 --> 00:04:15,318
And she told me, "It's your turn to fight."

76
00:04:15,318 --> 00:04:17,154
And my response?

77
00:04:17,154 --> 00:04:18,755
I'll go to law school

78
00:04:18,755 --> 00:04:23,093
to prevent this kind of ecocide from ever happening again.

79
00:04:23,093 --> 00:04:26,062
Now I did not know a lawyer,

80
00:04:26,062 --> 00:04:29,966
and I thought they were all rich and famous.

81
00:04:29,966 --> 00:04:31,434
But that's what I did.

82
00:04:31,434 --> 00:04:34,271
And I became my nation's general counsel,

83
00:04:34,271 --> 00:04:37,507
the first tribal member and woman to do so.

84
00:04:37,507 --> 00:04:38,708
Thank you.

85
00:04:38,708 --> 00:04:40,977
(Cheers and applause)

86
00:04:40,977 --> 00:04:46,249
And then I cofounded, with Molly Myers, a real tough Karuk activist,

87
00:04:46,249 --> 00:04:49,085
the Ridges to Riffles Indigenous Conservation Group.

88
00:04:49,085 --> 00:04:54,624
And we moved money and power to better serve the river.

89
00:04:54,624 --> 00:04:56,393
And that was important.

90
00:04:56,393 --> 00:05:00,230
The fish kill triggered our duties as Indigenous people

91
00:05:00,230 --> 00:05:05,502
to share the original instructions and work to renew the balance.

92
00:05:05,502 --> 00:05:10,307
Indigenous peoples on the Klamath were the first to call for dam removal.

93
00:05:10,307 --> 00:05:12,642
People thought it was impossible.

94
00:05:12,642 --> 00:05:16,880
A dam removal of this scale had never been done before.

95
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:18,715
Powerful corporations,

96
00:05:18,715 --> 00:05:23,520
some of the richest corporations and industries were against us.

97
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:28,291
They said removing the dams would release toxic sediment

98
00:05:28,291 --> 00:05:30,894
and destroy the river for good.

99
00:05:30,894 --> 00:05:33,496
The odds were against us.

100
00:05:33,496 --> 00:05:36,600
But we had something they did not.

101
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:38,802
The original instructions,

102
00:05:38,802 --> 00:05:42,339
thousands of years of traditional knowledge,

103
00:05:42,339 --> 00:05:43,907
ancient legal rights,

104
00:05:43,907 --> 00:05:48,812
vested in the US Constitution as the supreme law

105
00:05:48,812 --> 00:05:52,983
and our duties and responsibilities to the river, its creatures

106
00:05:52,983 --> 00:05:54,384
and our people,

107
00:05:54,384 --> 00:06:01,391
making the Un-Dam the Klamath Movement an unstoppable Indigenous force.

108
00:06:01,391 --> 00:06:03,059
Yeah. We protested.

109
00:06:03,059 --> 00:06:04,261
(Applause)

110
00:06:04,261 --> 00:06:05,762
There we go, guys, come on.

111
00:06:05,762 --> 00:06:07,597
(Cheers and applause)

112
00:06:07,597 --> 00:06:09,766
We protested.

113
00:06:09,766 --> 00:06:13,870
We built coalitions between Indigenous nations,

114
00:06:13,870 --> 00:06:16,373
the Karuk, the Hoopa, the Klamath tribes,

115
00:06:16,373 --> 00:06:21,311
commercial fishermen, NGOs and states and farmers.

116
00:06:21,311 --> 00:06:22,779
We litigated.

117
00:06:22,779 --> 00:06:25,482
We asserted the tribes’ reserved rights

118
00:06:25,482 --> 00:06:30,987
and combined them with modern environmental power and energy law.

119
00:06:30,987 --> 00:06:34,824
We won on almost every count.

120
00:06:34,824 --> 00:06:36,026
Yeah.

121
00:06:36,026 --> 00:06:38,561
(Cheers and applause)

122
00:06:38,561 --> 00:06:43,500
And the power company was required to install fish ladders

123
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:47,237
to continue to operate the dams.

124
00:06:47,237 --> 00:06:50,540
Empathy overcame fear.

125
00:06:50,540 --> 00:06:52,742
And perhaps for the first time,

126
00:06:52,742 --> 00:06:56,713
we shared the original instructions with the newcomers,

127
00:06:56,713 --> 00:07:00,083
and they accepted their shared responsibilities

128
00:07:00,083 --> 00:07:02,852
to work to renew the balance.

129
00:07:02,852 --> 00:07:05,488
With our allies and our former enemies,

130
00:07:05,488 --> 00:07:07,557
we found a common interest.

131
00:07:07,557 --> 00:07:13,396
It was cheaper to remove the dams than it was to install the fish ladders.

132
00:07:13,396 --> 00:07:15,198
Yeah, right?

133
00:07:15,198 --> 00:07:18,368
Yeah, there's that common interest.

134
00:07:18,368 --> 00:07:23,974
So we worked together to find a path forward,

135
00:07:23,974 --> 00:07:26,776
to restore the river and the balance.

136
00:07:26,776 --> 00:07:29,679
And the outcome was historic.

137
00:07:29,679 --> 00:07:34,184
A 500 million-dollar settlement agreement for dam removal

138
00:07:34,184 --> 00:07:36,853
that equally valued the rights of nature,

139
00:07:36,853 --> 00:07:39,456
business and Indigenous peoples.

140
00:07:39,456 --> 00:07:44,728
And it produced the world's largest salmon restoration project

141
00:07:44,728 --> 00:07:46,863
in world history.

142
00:07:46,863 --> 00:07:48,031
Yeah.

143
00:07:48,031 --> 00:07:50,266
Feel that, like, oh my God.

144
00:07:50,266 --> 00:07:56,539
And also the largest US dam removal project in history.

145
00:07:56,539 --> 00:07:59,909
In 2023 and 2024,

146
00:07:59,909 --> 00:08:03,480
we removed four dams on the Klamath River,

147
00:08:03,480 --> 00:08:08,418
and restoration across thousands of acres

148
00:08:08,418 --> 00:08:10,053
has already begun

149
00:08:10,053 --> 00:08:13,323
and will continue through 2028.

150
00:08:13,323 --> 00:08:16,059
I stood among tribal leaders

151
00:08:16,059 --> 00:08:20,697
when the first dam was breached through a series of explosions.

152
00:08:20,697 --> 00:08:23,700
The explosion released a freedom wave,

153
00:08:23,700 --> 00:08:27,137
a surge of water into a stretch of river

154
00:08:27,137 --> 00:08:30,607
that had been dewatered for more than a century.

155
00:08:30,607 --> 00:08:32,876
(Applause)

156
00:08:32,876 --> 00:08:36,279
The water moved with such force and confidence,

157
00:08:36,279 --> 00:08:39,182
knowing exactly where to flow and how to go,

158
00:08:39,182 --> 00:08:40,884
and it seemed to declare,

159
00:08:40,884 --> 00:08:42,652
"Do not call this a comeback,

160
00:08:42,652 --> 00:08:45,422
I have been here for years."

161
00:08:45,422 --> 00:08:49,192
(Cheers and applause)

162
00:08:49,192 --> 00:08:52,328
Later, at Iron Gate, the lowest dam,

163
00:08:52,328 --> 00:08:57,067
an excavator scooped some final dirt from the dam

164
00:08:57,067 --> 00:09:00,870
and the water began to go over the structure,

165
00:09:00,870 --> 00:09:02,772
first as a sparkle,

166
00:09:02,772 --> 00:09:04,641
then as a ribbon,

167
00:09:04,641 --> 00:09:09,079
and then the river reconnected with itself

168
00:09:09,079 --> 00:09:11,948
for the first time in 100 years,

169
00:09:11,948 --> 00:09:15,452
in a brilliant stream of prosperity.

170
00:09:15,452 --> 00:09:20,356
Three days later, the salmon came home

171
00:09:20,356 --> 00:09:26,029
and went past historical spawning grounds not accessed in 100 years.

172
00:09:26,029 --> 00:09:28,131
The salmon remembered.

173
00:09:28,131 --> 00:09:30,233
(Applause)

174
00:09:30,233 --> 00:09:33,069
They remembered.

175
00:09:33,069 --> 00:09:37,640
Many did not think that the river could heal,

176
00:09:37,640 --> 00:09:41,444
but its recovery has been remarkably rapid.

177
00:09:41,444 --> 00:09:43,480
A year after dam removal,

178
00:09:43,480 --> 00:09:46,649
the river is cleaner and colder and fiercer

179
00:09:46,649 --> 00:09:48,351
and smells sweeter

180
00:09:48,351 --> 00:09:51,721
than I have ever known it to be.

181
00:09:51,721 --> 00:09:54,858
And the salmon, they are literally jumping for joy.

182
00:09:54,858 --> 00:09:58,294
And if I wasn't wearing heels, I would jump for joy too.

183
00:09:58,294 --> 00:10:03,933
The first year, about 7,000 salmon passed the former dam site.

184
00:10:03,933 --> 00:10:08,872
In 2025, 13,000 salmon passed the former dam site

185
00:10:08,872 --> 00:10:12,809
and went further into their historical spawning grounds

186
00:10:12,809 --> 00:10:15,445
they'd been denied.

187
00:10:15,445 --> 00:10:18,715
The many Indigenous peoples of the Klamath,

188
00:10:18,715 --> 00:10:22,552
whose relationship with salmon had been severed by the dams,

189
00:10:22,552 --> 00:10:25,421
are welcoming the salmon home

190
00:10:25,421 --> 00:10:27,690
and returning to their homelands

191
00:10:27,690 --> 00:10:32,061
in historic, trend-setting land-back efforts.

192
00:10:32,061 --> 00:10:36,399
The Yurok tribe and many other tribes are employing tribal members

193
00:10:36,399 --> 00:10:37,800
and non-members,

194
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:42,772
providing living-wage jobs, restoring land in the river.

195
00:10:42,772 --> 00:10:49,445
These crews have planted -- get this -- 20 billion native seeds,

196
00:10:49,445 --> 00:10:53,550
bringing back -- yeah, yes, -- 20 billion native seeds.

197
00:10:53,550 --> 00:10:56,052
And look at these poppies, aren't they gorgeous?

198
00:10:56,052 --> 00:11:00,790
These new ecosystems are bringing back plants and animals,

199
00:11:00,790 --> 00:11:06,629
foods and medicines that anchor Indigenous cultures into the future.

200
00:11:06,629 --> 00:11:09,966
And these ecosystems have the potential

201
00:11:09,966 --> 00:11:17,307
to prevent 800,000 metric tons of CO2 a year over time.

202
00:11:17,307 --> 00:11:18,474
Yeah.

203
00:11:18,474 --> 00:11:20,877
(Applause)

204
00:11:20,877 --> 00:11:27,183
So I know many feel that there is no hope

205
00:11:27,183 --> 00:11:29,652
for a future on planet Earth

206
00:11:29,652 --> 00:11:34,390
because the Earth is too out of balance, too sick.

207
00:11:34,390 --> 00:11:37,293
And in that sense of urgency is real.

208
00:11:37,293 --> 00:11:39,162
But on the Klamath,

209
00:11:39,162 --> 00:11:48,638
the river and all life it supports are remembering how to be in balance.

210
00:11:48,638 --> 00:11:55,778
And this project has helped us remember an important truth.

211
00:11:55,778 --> 00:11:58,081
We can heal nature,

212
00:11:58,081 --> 00:12:03,086
and when we work with it, it works with us.

213
00:12:03,086 --> 00:12:07,023
And it starts with believing in yourself.

214
00:12:07,023 --> 00:12:11,761
So I want you all to remember that what you do matters.

215
00:12:11,761 --> 00:12:15,064
What you do with your life force makes a difference.

216
00:12:15,064 --> 00:12:19,269
Treat Indigenous rights not as obstacles to progress

217
00:12:19,269 --> 00:12:21,804
but as critical to it.

218
00:12:21,804 --> 00:12:27,010
Build mutually beneficial solutions to complex problems.

219
00:12:27,010 --> 00:12:31,814
Value ecosystem restoration as a strategic priority

220
00:12:31,814 --> 00:12:36,185
to the most pressing problems of our time.

221
00:12:36,185 --> 00:12:38,788
When we do that,

222
00:12:38,788 --> 00:12:41,190
balance is restored,

223
00:12:41,190 --> 00:12:44,494
life returns and so does hope.

224
00:12:44,494 --> 00:12:48,131
(Speaking in Yurok)

225
00:12:48,131 --> 00:12:49,365
Thank you.

226
00:12:49,365 --> 00:12:53,365
(Cheers and applause)
